+# Rank-Cut affine-certificate generator (arXiv:1305.2743)
+
+Status: deterministic construction and gates G1–G8 pass. The required oracle
+and G9 runs are **not complete** because OpenRouter returned HTTP 403, “Key limit
+exceeded (total limit),” on every script-controlled redraw. No oracle failure
+has been inferred from those errors, and this directory is not ready to submit.
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| native domain | combinatorics |
+| object regime | finite field |
+| computational core | graph |
+| certificate form | polynomial |
+| intuition | invariant: low-Hamming-weight endpoint XORs span a parity kernel |
+| domain essentiality | native |
+| reduction | none |
+
+## Problem and trust model
+
+The source is Guillemot and Marx, [*A faster FPT algorithm for Bipartite
+Contraction*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1305.2743). Section 2 defines simultaneous
+edge contraction and Lemma 2 equates a contraction set with a bipartite
+modulator of bounded graphic rank. Section 3 introduces the paper’s native
+**Rank-Cut** problem: find an `(X,Y)` edge cut whose `M`-rank is at most `k`.
+This generator uses Rank-Cut directly (`M` is empty); it does not compile the
+paper into SAT or a surrogate graph problem.
+
+The solver receives an undirected graph whose vertices have vectors in
+`F_2^d`. It must return the coefficients of an affine polynomial. The
+polynomial assigns each vertex a bit and thereby specifies every edge crossing
+between the two fibres. `verify` evaluates that polynomial exactly, rebuilds
+the edge set, computes graphic rank by union-find, deletes the cut, and checks
+terminal reachability. It accepts any polynomial witness meeting the stated
+bounds and never reads `inst["answer"]`.
+
+Generation is inverse: it samples a balanced affine polynomial first, makes
+two circulant pieces inside its fibres, joins them by a `k`-edge matching, and
+removes an internal matching so every vertex keeps the same degree. The planted
+crossing matching has rank exactly `k`, so the certificate is known before the
+finished instance exists.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+This is not a structural-hardness claim. Theorem 1 gives randomized
+`2^{O(k^2)} n m` and deterministic `2^{O(k^2)} n^{O(1)}` algorithms for
+Bipartite Contraction. Small `k` is precisely the paper’s FPT-easy regime and
+must not be hidden. Section 4, Lemma 5 also solves Constrained Rank-Cut using a
+weighted separator and at most `k` Ford–Fulkerson rounds.
+
+For this generated distribution, the successful reference algorithm is even
+simpler: unit-capacity Ford–Fulkerson finds the sparse terminal cut, and exact
+GF(2) elimination fits its affine polynomial. Across eight shipping instances
+it solved 8/8, with a measured median of **21,417 counted bit operations,
+3,244 residual-edge scans, and 0.000551 s**. That is easy with a program but not
+a credible unaided calculation over the displayed instance. The compact route
+uses the deliberately planted invariant: the short endpoint-XOR vectors span
+the codimension-one kernel. At shipping size its conservative count is 180
+edge XOR inspections, 23 kernel relations, and one constant evaluation: **204
+exact word operations**.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+The `demo` preset is genuinely hand-scale. For seed 7, `render` gives the
+following complete instance (the leftmost displayed label bit is `x_0`):
+
+```text
+Rank-Cut with an affine certificate
+n=24, d=7, k=2, X=[22], Y=[19], M=[]
+
+labels:
+0:0011011  1:1101011  2:1011010  3:0011001
+4:0010111  5:1111111  6:1000100  7:0111001
+8:0001110  9:1001110  10:0001100 11:1111000
+12:0110110 13:0000110 14:1111100 15:0010110
+16:0011101 17:0100110 18:0111000 19:0111010
+20:1111101 21:0010010 22:1011110 23:1101100
+
+edges:
+10-13 5-16 2-7 9-14 15-21 16-19 8-20 1-15 2-22 11-14 2-17 1-9
+4-10 17-22 19-21 0-2 11-23 3-13 5-13 7-23 1-19 8-17 8-12 0-7
+8-21 11-18 4-21 6-7 4-15 13-16 3-19 12-22 5-10 12-17 4-5 6-18
+10-15 11-20 1-3 6-23 0-22 14-20 9-20 18-23 3-16 9-12 0-6 14-18
+
+Return seven coefficient bits c_0...c_6, a bar, and b.
+```
+
+The answer is `<answer>0101010|0</answer>`, represented in JSON as
+`[0,1,0,1,0,1,0,0]`. The exact checks give:
+
+```python
+verify(inst, [0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0])
+# (True, "ok")
+verify(inst, [0, 1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0])
+# (False, "coefficient vector is too short")
+```
+
+A person can solve this demo by enumerating its 64 already-oriented affine
+polynomials or by finding the six-dimensional short-XOR kernel.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | vertices | label bits | k | regular degree | edges | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 24 | 7 | 2 | 4 | 48 | hand example; skipped by hardener |
+| easy | 60 | 24 | 4 | 6 | 180 | provisional shipping preset |
+| medium | 72 | 28 | 4 | 6 | 216 | available if oracle escalates |
+| hard | 88 | 32 | 4 | 6 | 264 | available if oracle escalates |
+
+The haystack grows while the witness stays a small affine polynomial. A doubled
+shipping instance builds with 120 vertices and 360 edges while retaining 25
+answer atoms.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | result | measured evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 12/12 planted checks and JSON round-trips |
+| G2 | pass | drop, swap, duplicate, empty, and out-of-field corruptions rejected with distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged answer recovered from prose/fence; garbage returned `None` |
+| G4 | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; language size 8,388,608 |
+| G5 | pass | shipping sampled density 0/200,000; demo exact count 1; coordinate descent 492 evaluations in 0.1574 s and failed |
+| G6 | pass | four attacks each 0/8; reference algorithm 8/8 |
+| G7 | pass | doubled instance verifies; 180 → 360 edges at fixed answer length |
+| G8 | pass | 80/80 composed invariance/witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 51 serialized chars, 13 estimated tokens, 25 atoms, 204 route operations |
+| G9(b) | **pending** | OpenRouter quota prevented a non-error oracle attempt |
+
+The G8 key is invariant under arbitrary vertex renumbering, edge reordering,
+GF(2) coordinate permutations and elementary changes of basis, XOR
+translation, and their compositions. Graph color refinement individualizes all
+generated vertices; label coordinates are then normalized against a canonical
+affine basis.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+| run | preset | solved / attempts | outcome |
+|---|---|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0 / 0 non-error attempts | blocked: all redraws HTTP 403 quota errors |
+| structural hint | easy | 0 / 0 | not run |
+| placebo hint | easy | 0 / 0 | not run |
+
+The hinted-minus-placebo diagnostic is therefore not defined yet. No
+conclusion about model difficulty or the intuition type is claimed until these
+script-owned runs complete. After quota restoration, run bare first, then run
+the two copied one-rung modules in separate scratch directories so their
+transcripts cannot overwrite one another.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import importlib.util
+
+spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location(
+    "g", "results/1305.2743/gen_1305_2743.py"
+)
+g = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
+spec.loader.exec_module(g)
+
+params = g.DIFFICULTY[g.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY]
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=123, **params)
+ok, reason = g.verify(inst, inst["answer"])
+assert (ok, reason) == (True, "ok")
+question = g.render(inst)
+```
+
+Once G9 passes, emit examples from the repository root with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 1305.2743 20 easy
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+The generator’s distribution is intentionally not hard for machines: max-flow
+plus elimination solves it in under a millisecond. Track B measures whether a
+model can discover and execute the compact invariant without tools. The
+0/200,000 guess result is an empirical upper-resolution statement under a
+uniform prior over affine polynomials that already separate the terminals; it
+does not prove there is only one shipping witness or describe a solver’s
+nonuniform prior. The demo alone was exhaustively counted and has one witness.
+
+The adversary panel tests equal-degree outliers, weight-at-most-two affine
+ansatzes, eight-restart coordinate descent, and 256 uniform restarts. It does
+not test SDP relaxations, learned graph partitioners, or every spectral
+variant. The successful min-cut reference is stronger and is disclosed rather
+than placed among failing attacks. The canonical form relies on color
+refinement individualizing generated graphs; its invariant fallback for
+hand-edited graphs with unresolved twins is not a complete isomorphism test.
+Finally, the three mandatory oracle arms remain the binding unfinished work;
+the present HTTP errors are infrastructure evidence, not hardness evidence.
